<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Black Stone</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as al-Hajar al-Aswad</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Black Stone is a stone set in the eastern corner of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaaba">Kaaba</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, revered by Muslims as dating back to Adam and Abraham and kissed or saluted by pilgrims as they circle the Kaaba. In 930 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qarmatians">Qarmatians</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a> sacked Mecca and carried it off to their base at al-Ahsa in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, returning it only in 951. Broken into fragments, it is now held in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> frame.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/black-stone.jpg" alt="Black Stone" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Black_stone.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
